CE, the son and successor of Nebuchadnezzar II.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/amelia-tokagahahau"><span class="enc-name">Amelia Tokagahahau</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Amelia Tokagahahau Aliki was Queen of Uvea, on Wallis Island in the Pacific, from 1869 until her death in 1895.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/amelia-tokagahahau-aliki"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/amelia-tokagahahau-aliki.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Amelia Tokagahahau Aliki</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Amelia Tokagahahau Aliki was queen, or Lavelua, of Uvea (Wallis Island) from 1869 until her death in 1895.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/amelia-umbria"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/amelia-umbria.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Amelia, Umbria</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Amelia, the ancient Ameria, is a hill town in southern Umbria, Italy.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/amenemhat-i"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/amenemhat-i.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Amenemhat I</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Amenemhat I was the founder of Egypt's Twelfth Dynasty, reigning for about thirty years from around 1985 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/amenemhat-ii"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/amenemhat-ii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Amenemhat II</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Amenemhat II was the third king of the Twelfth Dynasty of Egypt, reigning for more than thirty years around the turn of the twentieth and nineteenth centuries BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/amenemhat-iii"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/amenemhat-iii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Amenemhat III</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Amenemhat III was a pharaoh of the Twelfth Dynasty who reigned for about 45 years in the 19th century BCE, the height of the Middle Kingdom.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/amenemhat-iv"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/amenemhat-iv.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Amenemhat IV</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Amenemhat IV was the penultimate king of the Twelfth Dynasty of Egypt, reigning for about nine years in the late nineteenth century BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/amenhotep-huy"><span class="enc-name">Amenhotep Huy</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Amenhotep, called Huy, was the Egyptian viceroy of Kush, the 'King's Son of Kush', under Tutankhamun in the 14th century BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/amenhotep-iii"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/amenhotep-iii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Amenhotep III</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Amenhotep III was a pharaoh of the Eighteenth Dynasty of Egypt in the fourteenth century BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/amenirdis-i"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/amenirdis-i.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Amenirdis I</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Amenirdis I was a Kushite princess, daughter of King Kashta and sister of Piye and Shabaka, who served as God's Wife of Amun at Thebes in the late 8th century BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/america-the-beautiful"><span class="enc-name">America the Beautiful</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">In 1893 Katharine Lee Bates, a 33-year-old English professor at Wellesley College, took the train to Colorado Springs to teach at Colorado College.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/american-civil-war.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">American Civil War</span><span class="enc-type">War · 1861–1865</span><span class="enc-snip">The American Civil War was fought between the United States and the eleven southern states that seceded as the Confederacy to defend slavery.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/american-colonization-society"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/american-colonization-society.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">American Colonization Society</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">The American Colonization Society was an organisation founded in Washington in 1816 to settle free Black Americans in Africa.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/american-popular-revolutionary-alliance"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/american-popular-revolutionary-alliance.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">American Popular Revolutionary Alliance</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The American Popular Revolutionary Alliance (APRA) is a Peruvian political party founded by Víctor Raúl Haya de la Torre in Mexico in 1924 as an anti-imperialist…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/american-revolution"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/american-revolution.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">American Revolution</span><span class="enc-type">Revolution · 1765–1783</span><span class="enc-snip">The American Revolution was the rebellion of thirteen British colonies in North America, which declared independence in 1776 and won it in the war that ended with the…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/american-revolutionary-war.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">American Revolutionary War</span><span class="enc-type">War · 1775–1783</span><span class="enc-snip">The American Revolutionary War was fought between Britain and its rebel colonies in North America, which declared independence as the United States in 1776.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/american-south"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/american-south.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">American South</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The American South refers to the southern states of the United States, where plantation agriculture relied on enslaved African labour before the Civil War.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/americas"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/americas.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Americas</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Americas are the continents of North and South America and the Caribbean.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/americo-liberians"><span class="enc-name">Americo-Liberians</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">Americo-Liberians are Liberians descended from the free and formerly enslaved African Americans who settled Liberia from 1822, together with Africans freed from slave…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/amesbury-archer"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/amesbury-archer.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Amesbury Archer</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">The Amesbury Archer was an early Bronze Age man of the Bell Beaker culture whose grave was found in May 2002 at Amesbury, near Stonehenge in England.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ameyaw-kwaakye"><span class="enc-name">Ameyaw Kwaakye</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ameyaw Kwaakye is a king of the Akan state of Bono, in the forest-savanna borderland of present-day Ghana, remembered in oral tradition.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/amfissa"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/amfissa.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Amfissa</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Amfissa is a town in Phocis, central Greece, below Mount Parnassus near Delphi.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/amhara"><span class="enc-name">Amhara</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Amhara is a highland region in north-central Ethiopia, the homeland of the Amhara people.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/amharic"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/amharic.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Amharic</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">Amharic is a Semitic language of Ethiopia, written in the Ge'ez script, and the working language of the Ethiopian federal government.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/amhran-na-bhfiann"><span class="enc-name">Amhrán na bhFiann</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The song was first written in English as &quot;A Soldier's Song&quot;, &quot;early in 1910 or late in 1909&quot;, with words by Peadar Kearney and music by his childhood friend and…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/amida"><span class="enc-name">Amida</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Amida was a fortified city on the Tigris in upper Mesopotamia, now Diyarbakır in Turkey.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/amiens"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/amiens.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Amiens</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Amiens is a city on the Somme in northern France, the historic capital of Picardy.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/amilcar-cabral"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/amilcar-cabral.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Amílcar Cabral</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Amílcar Cabral was an agronomist, political thinker and anti-colonial leader from Portuguese Guinea and Cape Verde.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/al-amin"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/al-amin.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">al-Amin</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Al-Amin was the sixth Abbasid caliph, a son of Harun al-Rashid, who reigned from 809 to 813.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/amir"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/amir.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Amir</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">Amir, meaning commander or prince in Arabic, is a title used across the Islamic world for military commanders, governors and rulers.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/amir-title"><span class="enc-name">Amir (title)</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">Amir is an Arabic title meaning commander or prince, used for military and political leaders in the Islamic world.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/amir-al-muslimin"><span class="enc-name">Amir al-Muslimin</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">Amir al-Muslimin, meaning Commander of the Muslims, was the title adopted by the Almoravid rulers, notably Yusuf ibn Tashfin.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/amir-barid-i"><span class="enc-name">Amir Barid I</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Amir Barid I was the son of Qasim Barid and the second ruler of the Barid dynasty of Bidar in the Deccan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/amir-hamzah"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/amir-hamzah.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Amir Hamzah</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Amir Hamzah was an Indonesian poet and a member of the royal family of the Langkat Sultanate in North Sumatra.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/amir-husayn"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/amir-husayn.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Amir Husayn</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Amir Husayn was the leader of the Qara'unas, grandson of Qazaghan, who dominated the Western Chagatai Khanate in the 1360s.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/amir-ibn-abd-al-wahhab"><span class="enc-name">Amir ibn Abd al-Wahhab</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Amir ibn Abd al-Wahhab was the last major sultan of the Tahirid dynasty of Yemen, reigning from 1489 to 1517.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/amir-kabir"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/amir-kabir.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Amir Kabir</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Amir Kabir (Mirza Taqi Khan Farahani) was chief minister of Iran under Naser al-Din Shah from 1848 to 1851.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/amir-khan-of-tonk"><span class="enc-name">Amir Khan of Tonk</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Amir Khan of Tonk was a Pathan soldier of fortune who led a large army of mercenary cavalry in central India, allied with Yashwant Rao Holkar and plundering Rajput…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/amir-khusrau"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/amir-khusrau.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Amir Khusrau</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Amir Khusrau was a poet and musician of the Delhi Sultanate who served several sultans and was a disciple of the Sufi saint Nizamuddin Auliya.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/amira-dubbaj"><span class="enc-name">Amira Dubbaj</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Amira Dubbaj, also called Muzaffar Sultan, was the Ishaqid ruler of western Gilan, on the Caspian coast of Iran, in the early 16th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/amirids"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/amirids.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Amirids</span><span class="enc-type">State</span><span class="enc-snip">The Amirids were the family of al-Mansur (Almanzor), the chief minister who ruled the Caliphate of Córdoba in the name of Hisham II from the 970s.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/amiriya-madrasa"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/amiriya-madrasa.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Amiriya Madrasa</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Amiriya Madrasa is a religious school and mosque in Rada'a, Yemen, built by the Tahirid sultan Amir ibn Abd al-Wahhab and completed in 1504.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/amiternum"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/amiternum.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Amiternum</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Amiternum was an ancient Sabine town in the upper Aterno valley of central Italy, near modern L'Aquila.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/al-amjad-bahramshah"><span class="enc-name">Al-Amjad Bahramshah</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Al-Amjad Bahramshah was the Ayyubid emir of Baalbek from 1182 to 1230, son of Saladin's nephew Farrukh Shah.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/amlaib-conung"><span class="enc-name">Amlaíb Conung</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Amlaíb Conung was a Viking leader who, according to the Irish annals, arrived in Ireland in 853 and ruled the Norse of Dublin with Ímar.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/amlapura"><span class="enc-name">Amlapura</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Amlapura is a town in eastern Bali, Indonesia, and the capital of Karangasem Regency.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/amm"><span class="enc-name">Amm</span><span class="enc-type">Religion / idea</span><span class="enc-snip">Amm was the national god of the ancient South Arabian kingdom of Qataban in Yemen, often thought to be a moon god.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ammaedara"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ammaedara.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ammaedara</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Ammaedara was a Roman town in the west of present-day Tunisia, at modern Haïdra near the Algerian border.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/amman"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/amman.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Amman</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Amman is the capital and largest city of Jordan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/amman-citadel-inscription"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/amman-citadel-inscription.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Amman Citadel Inscription</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Amman Citadel Inscription is a fragmentary stone inscription in the Ammonite language, found in 1961 on the citadel hill of Amman, Jordan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ammi-saduqa"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ammi-saduqa.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ammi-saduqa</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ammi-saduqa was a king of the First Dynasty of Babylon, who ruled in 1646–1626 BCE by the High Middle Chronology or 1638–1618 BCE by the Low Middle Chronology.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ammianus-marcellinus"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ammianus-marcellinus.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ammianus Marcellinus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ammianus Marcellinus was a Greek-born Roman soldier and historian.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ammishtamru-i"><span class="enc-name">Ammishtamru I</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ammishtamru I was king of Ugarit in the fourteenth century BCE, during the Amarna period.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ammishtamru-ii"><span class="enc-name">Ammishtamru II</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ammishtamru II was king of Ugarit in the thirteenth century BCE, ruling as a vassal of the Hittite Empire at the height of the city's prosperity.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ammonius-saccas"><span class="enc-name">Ammonius Saccas</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ammonius Saccas was a Greek philosopher who taught in Alexandria in the first half of the third century CE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ammunira"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ammunira.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ammunira</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ammunira was the ruler of Beirut in the mid-14th century BCE, a vassal of the Egyptian pharaoh.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/amnisos"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/amnisos.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Amnisos</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Amnisos is an ancient harbour site on the north coast of Crete, east of Heraklion, that served as a port of Knossos.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/amnon-ben-tor"><span class="enc-name">Amnon Ben-Tor</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Amnon Ben-Tor is an Israeli archaeologist and professor at the Hebrew University of Jerusalem who has directed renewed excavations at Tel Hazor since 1990.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/amoghabhuti"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/amoghabhuti.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Amoghabhuti</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Amoghabhuti was a king of the Kunindas, a people of the Himalayan foothills, who reigned around the 1st century BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/amoghapasa"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/amoghapasa.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Amoghapasa</span><span class="enc-type">Religion / idea</span><span class="enc-snip">Amoghapasa is a form of the bodhisattva Avalokiteshvara in Mahayana and Vajrayana Buddhism, whose name refers to his unfailing noose for saving beings.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/amoghavarsha-i"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/amoghavarsha-i.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Amoghavarsha I</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Amoghavarsha I was the longest-reigning Rashtrakuta emperor, ruling the Deccan from 814 to 878.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/amoghavarsha-iii"><span class="enc-name">Amoghavarsha III</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Amoghavarsha III, also called Baddega, was Rashtrakuta emperor of the Deccan from about 936 to 939.</span></a></li></ul>
